Proposed risk.py change — Tier 3, NOT APPLIED. Needs God's explicit approval
and `RISK_REVIEW=1` to get past the pre-commit hook.

WHY
---
`check_symbol()` runs at bot startup (bot.py:274) against the config's symbol and
raises RiskBreach for anything outside ALLOWED_SYMBOLS. It blocks even
`--dry-run`, so the SOL supertrend leg cannot be paper-traded — let alone go
live — until SOL is on the list.

Everything else for that leg is built and tested: strategy/live_supertrend.py,
the bot_internals dispatch, config/params_sol_supertrend.yaml,
deploy/snapback-sol-supertrend.service, and tools/supertrend_parity.py (119/119
backtest entries reproduced, 0 spurious, 0 premature exits). This one line is
the remaining gate.

WHAT IT WIDENS, HONESTLY
------------------------
This is the allowlist that has kept every leg on a single, deeply liquid
instrument since v1. Adding SOL means:

  * Any leg whose config names SOL/USDT:USDT can now trade — not just this one.
    `config/params_cnh_hybrid_short_sol.yaml` also names SOL and would become
    runnable. That leg measured WR 61% but CAGR 1.3% with 1,360 days underwater
    (SOL_LEG_VERDICT.md round 5) and should NOT be funded. The allowlist stops
    being the thing that prevents it; only not-enabling its unit does.
  * SOL is ~3-4x more volatile than BTC (4h ATR 2.33% of price vs BTC's ~1%),
    with thinner depth. Slippage and gap risk past the SL are correspondingly
    worse than every backtest here models.
  * exchange/constraints.py is BTC-specific (min_qty_btc, min_notional_usdt=50,
    price_step=0.1) and merge_with_live keeps the TIGHTER value, so SOL inherits
    BTC's $50 min-notional and $0.10 tick. Safe (it only over-constrains), but
    it is why minimum capital is $200 rather than ~$25.

MAX_NOTIONAL_USD = 500 is NOT touched by this patch and stays the real backstop:
whatever the allowlist says, no single order can exceed $500 notional.

